  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}=0\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2({x}_{2}-1)^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =-({x}_{1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-2, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{2}^{2} & =-{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${y}_{1}$-axis.",
